    tldr: "A docking site for the hormone gastrin, found at very high numbers on medullary thyroid cancer cells. Minigastrin-like radioactive drugs can bind it to find or treat that cancer, though they also light up the stomach because the same receptor lines it normally.",
    summary: "CCK2R is a G-protein-coupled receptor for the hormones gastrin and cholecystokinin, expressed physiologically in the gastric mucosa where it regulates acid secretion, and in parts of the central nervous system. It is overexpressed at very high incidence and density in medullary thyroid carcinoma (MTC) - historically detected indirectly through the pentagastrin stimulation test - and also occurs in a substantial minority of small-cell lung cancers, astrocytomas, stromal ovarian cancers, gastrointestinal stromal tumours and some gastroenteropancreatic neuroendocrine tumours.\n\nRadiolabelled analogues of minigastrin, which retains the C-terminal receptor-binding tetrapeptide sequence (Trp-Met-Asp-Phe-NH2) common to gastrin and CCK, have been developed for both imaging (111In-, 99mTc-, or 68Ga-labelled minigastrin analogues, including Demogastrin 2 and MGS5) and therapy (90Y- or 177Lu-labelled minigastrin, such as 177Lu-PP-F11N). In patients with metastatic MTC, CCK2R-targeted imaging has detected more lesions than somatostatin receptor imaging, reflecting the low SSTR expression typical of MTC.\n\nAs a target, CCK2R's strength is very high density (over 90% of MTCs) and no competing standard-of-care radiotracer. Its central weakness, which has so far limited therapeutic use, is that the stomach expresses CCK2R physiologically and receives a comparable or higher radiation dose than the tumour with agonist-based minigastrin analogues, making the stomach dose-limiting; kidney and bone-marrow toxicity have also constrained early therapy trials, and receptor antagonist-based ligands (for example those built on nastorazepide) are being explored as an alternative with potentially better tumour-to-stomach ratios.",
