    id: "upar",
    kind: "target",
    name: "uPAR (urokinase plasminogen activator receptor)",
    aka: ["uPAR", "PLAUR"],
    symbol: "PLAUR",
    tldr: "A protein that helps cancer cells break through surrounding tissue and spread. Higher amounts on a tumour are linked to more aggressive disease, and a PET tracer built on a small peptide can measure how much of it a tumour has.",
    summary: "uPAR is a glycosylphosphatidylinositol-anchored cell-surface receptor for urokinase-type plasminogen activator (uPA); the uPA/uPAR system focuses proteolytic activity (activation of plasmin, which degrades extracellular matrix) at the cell surface, driving tumour cell invasion, matrix remodelling and metastasis. uPAR overexpression is an established biomarker of tumour aggressiveness across several common cancers, including prostate, breast, bladder and glioma, and correlates with independent prognostic information beyond PSA and Gleason score in prostate cancer.\n\nThe PET ligand 68Ga-NOTA-AE105 (and the earlier 64Cu-DOTA-AE105), based on the high-affinity peptide antagonist AE105, has been evaluated in first-in-human and phase 2 studies in prostate cancer, breast cancer, bladder cancer and glioma. In localised prostate cancer, tumour SUVmax on 68Ga-NOTA-AE105 PET correlated significantly with Gleason score (Spearman ρ = 0.55), and a SUVmax cut-off achieved 96% sensitivity for detecting Gleason score ≥3+4, raising the possibility of uPAR PET reducing the number of repeat biopsies needed in active surveillance. In glioma, uPAR-PET uptake was associated with worse overall and progression-free survival.\n\nAs a target, uPAR's strength is a genuine link to tumour aggressiveness and metastatic potential across multiple tumour types, giving it prognostic as well as localising value. Its main limitation to date is that all published clinical PET data come from small studies run predominantly by one research group, so multicentre validation is still needed, and no uPAR-targeted therapeutic radioligand has reached clinical use.",
