    name: "Mesothelin (MSLN)",
    aka: ["MSLN"],
    symbol: "MSLN",
    tldr: "A protein normally found only on the thin lining of the chest and abdomen, but made in large amounts by mesothelioma and by most pancreatic and many ovarian cancers. Because healthy organs barely make it, it is an attractive handle for antibody-based imaging and treatment.",
    summary: "Mesothelin is a glycosylphosphatidylinositol-anchored cell-surface glycoprotein whose normal expression is restricted to mesothelial cells lining the pleura, pericardium and peritoneum. Its physiological function is not fully established, but it binds CA-125/MUC16, an interaction implicated in the peritoneal spread of ovarian cancer, and its overexpression has been linked to activation of NF-κB, MAPK and PI3K signalling that promotes proliferation, invasion and resistance to apoptosis.\n\nMesothelin is essentially absent from normal pancreas but is expressed in close to 100% of pancreatic ductal adenocarcinomas and virtually all epithelioid (though not sarcomatoid) mesotheliomas, and in a substantial majority of ovarian carcinomas (particularly serous subtype) and roughly half of lung adenocarcinomas, giving a wide tumour-to-normal-tissue contrast that has made it a target for antibody-drug conjugates (for example DMOT4039A/anetumab), immunotoxins (SS1P, LMB-100), CAR-T therapy and radiolabelled antibodies. 89Zr-labelled anti-mesothelin antibody immunoPET (for example 89Zr-MMOT0530A) has visualised pancreatic and ovarian cancer lesions in patients, with higher mean tumour SUVmax in ovarian than pancreatic lesions, plausibly reflecting the dense desmoplastic stroma that limits antibody penetration in pancreatic tumours. A thorium-227-labelled anti-mesothelin antibody-chelator conjugate (BAY2287411) has been tested in a first-in-human alpha-therapy trial in mesothelin-expressing tumours.\n\nAs a target, mesothelin's strength is a genuinely restricted normal-tissue expression pattern combined with near-universal expression in several hard-to-treat cancers. Its main limitations are that, as an antibody target, achieving good tumour penetration in stroma-rich tumours such as pancreatic adenocarcinoma is harder than in mesothelioma or ovarian cancer, and mesothelin is also shed from the cell surface into blood and ascites, which can act as an antigen sink for circulating antibody-based agents.",
