    symbol: "TPBG",
    tldr: "A protein the placenta makes during pregnancy that many cancers switch back on while most healthy adult tissue does not. Antibody-drug conjugates against it have been tested in patients, and since 2024 the first radiolabelled versions have reached people in China.",
    summary: "5T4, encoded by TPBG (HGNC:12004), is a 72 kD heavily N-glycosylated membrane protein with leucine-rich repeats, first found by searching for surface molecules shared between human trophoblast and cancer cells. It is expressed by many different cancers but rarely in normal adult tissue, and its expression is linked to epithelial-mesenchymal transition, CXCL12/CXCR4 chemotaxis and a tumour-initiating phenotype (Stern and Harrop, Cancer Immunol Immunother 2017).\n\nThe target was validated clinically as an antibody-drug conjugate first. Pfizer's PF-06263507, an anti-5T4 IgG1 carrying monomethylauristatin-F, was given to 26 patients in a first-in-human trial (NCT01891669): the maximum tolerated dose was 4.34 mg/kg, ocular toxicity caused the dose-limiting events, and no objective responses were seen in patients who were not selected for 5T4 expression. In mesothelioma, 5T4 was expressed in 10 of 12 cell lines and most biopsies, and antibodies and ADCs against it internalise into lysosomes.\n\nRadiopharmaceutical work is recent and largely Chinese. A humanised single-domain antibody, [68Ga]Ga-NOTA-H006, went from camel immunisation to a pilot clinical study at Huashan Hospital, Fudan University, registered as NCT06162988, and mapped 5T4-positive lesions in patients with a mean effective dose of 0.034 mSv/MBq (EJNMMI 2025). An [18F]AlF-RESCA-H006 version was built for pancreatic adenocarcinoma, and a full-antibody theranostic pair, [89Zr]Zr-DFO-HB5 for imaging with [177Lu]Lu- and [161Tb]Tb-DOTA-HB5 for therapy, slowed tumour growth in two colorectal cancer xenograft models (J Med Chem 2026). A 2026 systematic review lists 5T4 with CEACAM5, TROP2, GPC3 and ROR1 as oncofetal surface antigens in advanced prostate cancer, including neuroendocrine and castration-resistant disease.\n\nThe open question is patient selection: the only ADC trial enrolled unselected patients and saw no responses, which is the gap a 5T4 imaging agent could close.",
