    tldr: "A receptor that pulls molecules into cells and is found at high levels in several cancers, including ovarian, endometrial and triple-negative breast cancer. A peptide-drug conjugate against it is in a phase 1 trial, and the first radioactive pair (one version for scans, one for treatment) was published in 2026.",
    summary: "Sortilin, encoded by SORT1 (HGNC:11186), is a sorting receptor that carries bound ligands such as neurotensin and progranulin into cells. That internalising function is what drug developers exploit. Theratechnologies built TH19P01, a peptide that needs sortilin to enter cells, and conjugated it to docetaxel as TH1902 (sudocetaxel zendusortide). In their preclinical work, high sortilin expression correlated with shorter survival in triple-negative breast cancer, more than 75% of clinically annotated ovarian and endometrial tumours showed high sortilin by immunohistochemistry, and TH1902 outperformed unconjugated docetaxel in xenografts without causing neutropenia. The phase 1 trial in advanced solid tumours, NCT04706962, started in March 2021 and is listed as active, not recruiting.\n\nThe radiopharmaceutical side is new. In March 2026 a Peking University group labelled the anti-sortilin antibody latozinemab to make [89Zr]Zr-DFO-latozinemab for PET and [177Lu]Lu-DOTA-latozinemab for therapy. Melanoma tissue showed high sortilin (mean H-score 189.79); the PET agent gave high-contrast tumour images peaking at 72 hours; the lutetium conjugate reached 39.08% injected dose per gram in tumour at 72 hours; and 11.1 MBq significantly slowed tumour growth. The authors describe it as the first sortilin-directed theranostic pair.\n\nWhat is missing is anything between a full antibody with multi-day kinetics and the peptide carrying a chemotherapy payload: no small-molecule or peptide sortilin radioligand, and no human imaging data, has been published.",
