    aka: ["PSD-95", "PSD95", "SAP90", "postsynaptic density protein 95", "DLG4"],
    symbol: "DLG4",
    tldr: "The main scaffold protein on the receiving side of brain synapses. Losing synapses tracks memory decline in Alzheimer's disease, but today's synapse PET scans only see the sending side (SV2A). No tracer for PSD-95 has been published, so the receiving side cannot yet be imaged.",
    summary: "DLG4 (HGNC:2903) encodes postsynaptic density protein 95, the scaffold that anchors glutamate receptors in the postsynaptic density of excitatory synapses. Synapse loss is an early event in Alzheimer's disease and is closely linked to cognitive decline, and PSD-95 is one of the proteins that falls. In post-mortem brain from the Banner Sun Health Research Institute, PSD-95 was lower in every region studied in Alzheimer's dementia than in controls without dementia (not significant in the hippocampus), and PSD-95 levels correlated with tangle and plaque density and with Mini Mental State Examination scores. In cerebrospinal fluid, a Massachusetts General Hospital study of two cohorts (178 and 156 people) found PSD-95 raised in Alzheimer's disease but not specific to it.\n\nSynaptic density PET today images the presynaptic vesicle protein SV2A with tracers such as 11C-UCB-J, used for example to follow synapse loss in animal models. A PubMed search on 24 September 2026 for PSD-95 or DLG4 together with PET, SPECT, radioligand or radiotracer returned 17 records, and none described a radioligand that binds PSD-95 itself.\n\nThat absence is the point of this record. PSD-95 is an intracellular scaffold rather than a receptor, so a tracer would need to cross the blood-brain barrier and the cell membrane and bind a protein-protein interaction domain, which is harder than binding a vesicle protein. The unmet need is a postsynaptic counterpart to SV2A PET that could separate loss of receiving sites from loss of transmitting sites.",
